WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:08.320
In the first film, growth factors and amino acids switched mTORC1 on at the

2
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:13.900
lysosome. But what stops a cell from building when its ATP starts running low?

3
00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:18.820
Running out of cellular ATP threatens survival fast.

4
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.940
So a second master kinase, AMPK, serves as the cell's energy gauge.

5
00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:29.453
You might expect a fuel gauge to watch ATP itself.

6
00:00:30.673 --> 00:00:36.433
The catch is that a working cell holds ATP nearly steady, so ATP barely budges at

7
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:43.713
first. Instead, the enzyme adenylate kinase balances two ADP against one ATP plus

8
00:00:43.713 --> 00:00:48.353
one AMP. So AMP tracks ADP squared, divided by ATP.

9
00:00:49.793 --> 00:00:53.773
When ATP dips just ten percent, AMP rises almost fourfold.

10
00:00:54.883 --> 00:00:59.463
AMP works like a low-fuel warning light that trips while the needle has barely

11
00:00:59.463 --> 00:01:06.823
moved. AMPK has three subunits: alpha carries the kinase, and beta holds the complex

12
00:01:06.823 --> 00:01:13.083
together. The gamma subunit holds pockets where AMP and ATP compete, so it reads

13
00:01:13.083 --> 00:01:19.483
their ratio. Full activation needs a phosphate on alpha, added mainly by an

14
00:01:19.483 --> 00:01:22.123
always-active kinase called LKB1.

15
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.920
If LKB1 is always active, why does AMPK stay quiet in a rested cell?

16
00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:33.400
Because phosphatases keep stripping that phosphate back off.

17
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:40.580
When AMP binds gamma, AMPK shifts shape so phosphatases cannot easily remove the

18
00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:46.420
phosphate. AMP also adds a direct allosteric boost, switching the kinase strongly

19
00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:47.420
on.

20
00:01:47.883 --> 00:01:49.983
Now put both gauges in one cell.

21
00:01:51.403 --> 00:01:57.963
Growth factors and amino acids are plentiful, but ATP dips and AMPK switches on, and

22
00:01:57.963 --> 00:02:02.823
it vetoes growth. One veto route runs through the TSC brake.

23
00:02:04.103 --> 00:02:10.063
So test it. Suppose a cell has lost TSC2, a key part of that brake.

24
00:02:11.363 --> 00:02:14.043
Can AMPK still shut the growth kinase down?

25
00:02:20.017 --> 00:02:23.817
Partly, yes, because AMPK carries two separate brakes.

26
00:02:25.037 --> 00:02:31.497
Normally it phosphorylates TSC2 at activating sites, turning the TSC brake back on

27
00:02:31.497 --> 00:02:33.297
and draining Rheb of GTP.

28
00:02:34.717 --> 00:02:41.197
But AMPK also phosphorylates Raptor, a part of mTORC1 itself, braking the complex

29
00:02:41.197 --> 00:02:47.697
directly. So even without TSC2, energy stress can still dim protein building.

30
00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:53.370
At the same time, AMPK rebalances the cell's energy budget.

31
00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:58.530
It cuts spending by switching off enzymes that build fat and cholesterol.

32
00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:05.150
The first fat-building enzyme also makes a blocker of fatty acid entry into

33
00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:10.550
mitochondria. Switching it off opens that door, so fat is burned for fuel.

34
00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:17.330
In muscle, AMPK brings more glucose transporters to the surface to raise fuel

35
00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:18.330
income.

36
00:03:18.833 --> 00:03:24.553
So AMPK reads rising AMP to conserve energy and veto mTORC1.

37
00:03:26.033 --> 00:03:31.253
Gamma senses AMP against ATP, while ADP and calcium signals can also tune this

38
00:03:31.253 --> 00:03:37.473
switch. That energy veto overrides both growth yeses whenever fuel runs short.

39
00:03:38.773 --> 00:03:42.473
Protecting cellular energy comes before building new protein.
